// ============================================================================
// unified.z  --  one declaration form for module / struct / func / sum / shape
// ============================================================================
//
// THE RULE
//
//     Name <compile-time args> (runtime args) -> projection { body }
//
// Every declaration takes args and builds a block of named values.
// The projection says what escapes the block:
//
//     -> ()        nothing escapes            procedure
//     -> T         one value escapes          function
//     -> self      all of the block           struct / module / shape
//     -> {a, b}    these members of it        multiple return, AND visibility
//     -> oneof     one member of it, tagged   sum
//
// APPLYING A DECLARATION. Stated because everything else depends on it:
//
//     List           the declaration
//     List<u8>       ...compile-time args applied   ->  a TYPE
//     List<u8>(mem)  ...runtime args applied        ->  a VALUE
//
//     No runtime group at all (`x :: -> T {...}`) means the thing IS its
//     value, already evaluated. An explicit empty `()` means you call it.
//     That is the whole of why `Heap()` is a call and `Writer` is not.
//
// BRACKETS
//     <>   compile-time args      List<u8>, ptr<T>
//     ()   runtime args           Heap()
//     []   indexing               xs[0]
//
// BINDING.  `:` is type ascription and nothing else. The binder is what
// carries meaning, and a hole is the absence of one.
//
//     name          :: v     compile-time binding
//     name : T      :: v     ...with the type spelled out
//     name          := v     runtime binding
//     name : T      := v     ...with the type spelled out
//     name : T               no binder  ->  HOLE
//     name          := undefined        a field you fill in later
//
//     `=` is assignment: mutating an existing binding, filling a declared
//     hole in a literal, a default in a parameter list, or passing a
//     compile-time arg by name (`to_string<F = hex_u8>`). It never creates
//     a binding.
//
//     A runtime param of a `-> self` def IS a field: the block is what you
//     kept, so the params are in it. A def whose body would be empty may drop
//     it -- `Span :: (lo: u32, hi: u32) -> self` is a record declaration.
//     A field bound with `::` costs zero bytes; it lives in the type.
//
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// HOLES.  `name : T` with no binder is a hole. What a hole MEANS is decided by
// the projection, and that single fact is the whole of both sums and
// interfaces:
//
//     -> self    CONJUNCTIVE. All holes must be filled to instantiate. A block
//                with an unfilled hole cannot be instantiated, so it is usable
//                only as a bound or behind a pointer. That is all an interface
//                ever was, and it applies to every block equally -- a List with
//                one unfilled field is abstract for the same reason Allocator
//                is. Filling the holes gives you a value.
//
//     -> oneof   DISJUNCTIVE. Exactly one hole is filled. A sum is never
//                abstract; there is nothing left to fill.
//
// So `Allocator` and `Option` are the same declaration shape read two ways.
// THERE IS NO `trait`, `interface`, `abstract`, `enum` OR `union`.
//
// The projection quantifies over HOLES and nothing else. `::` and `:=` members
// are unaffected by it, so a sum carries methods exactly as a struct does:
//
//                       -> self                    -> oneof
//     name : T          requirement                variant
//     name :: v         method / ct field          method
//     name := v         runtime field              -- (no shared storage)
//
// Construction needs no new rule: `=` already fills a declared hole in a
// literal, so `Option<u8>{ some = 3 }`. Since a variant is a hole of type T,
// the variant name is also a derived function (T) -> Self: `Option<u8>.some(3)`.
//
// `-> oneof {a, b}` is a sum over a subset -- variants only the declaring
// module can construct, matchable by everyone. It falls out of visibility
// below rather than being designed.
//
// On the name: `-> T` is already glossed "one value escapes", so `-> one`
// would collide with the projection above it, and a bare `one` in a slot that
// otherwise holds type names burns a plausible identifier. `union` is poisoned
// by C, `variant` names a member rather than the whole, `either` implies two.
// It is spelled bare and not `-> oneof self` because a sum does not project the
// whole block -- one member escapes, which puts it in `{a, b}`'s family.
//
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// VISIBILITY IS THE PROJECTION. `-> self` exports the whole block; `-> {a, b}`
// exports two names. There is no `pub` and no export list, because the
// projection always was one. See `io` below.
//
// A value may escape while its type does not. `io.out` is exported and
// `FileWriter` is not, which is an opaque type for free.
//
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// KEYWORDS.  `self` is the only new one in the declaration form. It always
// means "the block".
//     -> self               in a projection: all of the block escapes
//     self                  a receiver, by value
//     &self                 a receiver, by pointer
//     ptr<self>             what &self desugars to
//     -> Result<self, E>    a projection may WRAP self: fallible construction
//
// The rest of the budget, written down so it stops growing by accident:
//     oneof                 a projection
//     match  _              destructuring
//     try                   propagate the `err` arm of a Result
//     defer                 run at scope exit
//     if while for loop return continue break
//
// There is no `struct`, `enum`, `union`, `interface`, `trait`, `impl`,
// `operator`, `pub`, `dyn`, or `import`. Imports are ordinary compile-time
// bindings of an ordinary compile-time function: `std :: import("std")`.
//
// MATCH.  An arm is spelled like a declaration -- a name, a runtime group
// binding the payload, a body. Nothing new to learn.
//
//     match (v) {
//         none     { ... }
//         some (x) { ... }
//         _        { ... }
//     }
//
// `_` is a hole in a BINDING position: a wildcard arm, an ignored payload, or
// a compile-time arg you decline to name (`ptr<List<T, _>>`). One symbol, one
// idea.
//
// OPERATORS ARE MEMBERS WITH SYMBOLIC NAMES. There is no `operator` keyword.
//     [] :: (&self, i: u32) -> T       indexing
//     () :: (&self, v: u8)  -> ()      calling
// A closure is therefore not a special form. It is a block with a `()` member,
// which is why `Fn<A, R>` below is an ordinary shape and needs no function-type
// machinery. See NOTE 6.
//
// POINTERS
//     ptr<T>       typed
//     ptr          the same thing with its arg defaulted to opaque. Not a
//                  special form -- compile-time args take defaults anyway.
//
// COMPILE-TIME ARGS COME IN TWO KINDS
//     <T>                      inferred from a runtime argument's type
//     <F: Formattable<T> = ?>  solved by search: nothing at the call site
//                              mentions it, so the solver goes and finds it
//     Either may be passed by name at the call site with `=`.
//
// A DECLARATION WITH NO NAME is what the solver searches. `::` with nothing
// on its left is an anonymous compile-time binding; it goes in the pool.
// Give it a name and it is reachable only by hand.
//
// A TYPE IN VALUE POSITION IS THE SOLVER'S DEFAULT INSTANCE. `Ord<u8>.compare`
// means "whatever instance the solver picks for Ord<u8>". This is not sugar --
// it is what lets a derived instance recurse into itself, and what a named
// instance calls to delegate. See `reverse_u8`.
//
// UFCS.  `a.f(x)` falls back to `f(a, x)` when `a` has no member `f`, and `f`
// is found by ORDINARY NAME LOOKUP IN THE CALLER'S SCOPE. That is precisely
// why an extrinsic function may never satisfy a shape -- see NOTE 4.
//
// TWO KINDS OF ABSTRACTION, ONE SYNTAX. See NOTE 1.
//     holes take self      ->  SHAPE.      Intrinsic to a type, matched
//                              structurally, coercible to ptr<Shape>.
//     holes do not         ->  DICTIONARY. Extrinsic, several may exist, the
//                              caller picks, the solver defaults it.
//     Mixing the two in one block is an error, not a third thing.
//
// A SHAPE NAME IN AN ARGUMENT POSITION says which dispatch you are buying:
//     <M: Allocator>   a bound.   Monomorphised, calls direct, 0-byte field.
//     (m: Allocator)   ptr<Allocator>. Erased, calls indirect.
// The bracket already separates compile time from run time; it separates
// static from dynamic for the same reason, and no `dyn` is needed.
//
// ============================================================================


// ============================================================================
// std.core
// ============================================================================

Option :: <T> -> oneof {
    none: ()
    some: T
}

Result :: <T, E> -> oneof {
    ok:  T
    err: E
}

// `try e` yields the `ok` payload, or returns the `err` arm to the caller's
// projection. It is the only reason `Result` is worth having over a match.

// A closure is a block with a `()` member, so a callable is an ordinary shape.
// One per arity is the cost; see OPEN.
Fn  :: <A, R>    -> self { (): (&self, a: A)       -> R }
Fn2 :: <A, B, R> -> self { (): (&self, a: A, b: B) -> R }


// ============================================================================
// std.mem
// ============================================================================

// Nothing but holes -- two names with types and no binder. Uninstantiable,
// therefore usable only as a bound or behind a pointer.
Allocator :: -> self {
    alloc: (&self, n: u32) -> ptr
    free:  (&self, p: ptr) -> ()
}

// The typed layer is an ordinary module of free functions. It is not part of
// the shape, so anyone can extend it, and UFCS makes it read like a method.
mem :: -> self {
    make :: <T, M: Allocator> (m: M, n: u32) -> ptr<T> { m.alloc(n * size_of<T>()) }
    drop :: <T, M: Allocator> (m: M, p: ptr<T>) -> ()  { m.free(p) }
}

// No fields -> zero-sized -> the field costs 0 bytes wherever it is stored
// and every call through it is direct.
Heap :: () -> self {
    alloc :: (&self, n: u32) -> ptr { c_malloc(n) }
    free  :: (&self, p: ptr) -> ()  { c_free(p)   }
}

Arena :: (base: ptr<u8>, size: u32) -> self {
    top := 0
    alloc :: (&self, n: u32) -> ptr {
        p := self.base + self.top
        self.top += n
        p
    }
    free :: (&self, p: ptr) -> () { }
}

// Wraps any allocator, and is itself one.
Tracking :: <M: Allocator> (inner: M) -> self {
    alloc :: (&self, n: u32) -> ptr { log("alloc %", n); self.inner.alloc(n) }
    free  :: (&self, p: ptr) -> ()  { log("free");       self.inner.free(p)  }
}


// ============================================================================
// std.io  --  the projection is the export list
// ============================================================================

Writer :: -> self {
    write_bytes: (&self, p: ptr<u8>, n: u32) -> ()
}

io :: -> {out, err, write} {

    // Not in the projection, so it does not escape. `out` does, which makes
    // its type opaque outside this block at no cost.
    FileWriter :: (fd: i32) -> self {
        write_bytes :: (&self, p: ptr<u8>, n: u32) -> () { c_write(self.fd, p, n) }
    }

    out :: FileWriter(1)
    err :: FileWriter(2)

    write :: <W: Writer> (w: W, s: slice<u8>) -> () { w.write_bytes(s.ptr, s.len) }
}


// ============================================================================
// std.list  --  methods live in the block. See NOTE 4.
// ============================================================================

// `M: Allocator` means "any type structurally providing that shape".
// ptr<Allocator> provides it too -- that is the entire static/dynamic switch,
// with no keyword. See NOTE 2.
List :: <T, M: Allocator> (mem: M) -> self {
    capacity := 10
    count    := 0

    // A field initialiser sees the runtime params directly; methods reach them
    // through `self`. `make` itself is found by lookup in this scope, per UFCS.
    items    := mem.make<T>(capacity)     // UFCS -> make<T>(mem, capacity)

    push :: (&self, item: T) -> () {
        if (self.count + 1 > self.capacity) { self.expand() }
        self.items[self.count] = item
        self.count += 1
    }

    // Returning `T` was a lie about the empty list. This is what `-> oneof`
    // buys in the first ten lines of a standard library.
    pop :: (&self) -> Option<T> {
        if (self.count == 0) { return Option<T>.none() }
        self.count -= 1
        Option<T>.some(self.items[self.count])
    }

    expand :: (&self) -> () {
        new_cap   := self.capacity * 2
        new_items := self.mem.make<T>(new_cap)
        for (i := 0; i < self.count; i += 1) { new_items[i] = self.items[i] }
        self.mem.drop(self.items)
        self.items    = new_items
        self.capacity = new_cap
    }

    deinit :: (&self) -> () {
        self.mem.drop(self.items)
        self.items = nil
        self.count = 0
    }

    [] :: (&self, i: u32) -> T { self.items[i] }
}


// ============================================================================
// std.ord  --  Ord is a DICTIONARY: u8 has no one true ordering, and the
// caller must be able to pick. Nothing is attached to u8.
// ============================================================================

Ord :: <T> -> self {
    compare: (a: T, b: T) -> i32     // no self -> dictionary
}

// Anonymous -> in the solver pool. This is what `Ord<u8>` resolves to.
:: -> Ord<u8> {
    compare = (a, b) -> i32 {
        if (a < b) { return -1 }
        if (a > b) { return  1 }
        return 0
    }
}

// Named -> never searched. Reachable only by hand. `Ord<u8>` on the right is
// the pooled instance, not this one -- a type in value position is what the
// solver picks.
reverse_u8 :: -> Ord<u8> {
    compare = (a, b) -> i32 { Ord<u8>.compare(b, a) }
}

// `_` for the allocator: sort does not care which one, and naming a parameter
// only so that `List<T, M>` parses is the noisiest thing in the old draft.
sort :: <T, O: Ord<T> = ?> (xs: ptr<List<T, _>>) -> () {
    for (i := 1; i < xs.count; i += 1) {
        v := xs.items[i]
        j := i
        while (j > 0 && O.compare(xs.items[j - 1], v) > 0) {
            xs.items[j] = xs.items[j - 1]
            j -= 1
        }
        xs.items[j] = v
    }
}


// ============================================================================
// std.fmt  --  also a DICTIONARY: you want hex and decimal for the same u8.
// ============================================================================

Formattable :: <T> -> self {
    format: (v: T, w: ptr<Writer>) -> ()
}

:: -> Formattable<u8> {
    format = (v, w) -> () {
        if (v >= 10) { Formattable<u8>.format(v / 10, w) }
        d := '0' + (v % 10)
        w.write_bytes(&d, 1)
    }
}

hex_u8 :: -> Formattable<u8> {
    format = (v, w) -> () {
        digits :: "0123456789abcdef"
        hi := digits[v / 16]
        lo := digits[v % 16]
        w.write_bytes(&hi, 1)
        w.write_bytes(&lo, 1)
    }
}

// A derived instance is an ordinary def: args on the left, result on the
// right. The solver matches the return type. No special impl syntax, no
// pattern on the left-hand side. See NOTE 5.
:: <T, M, F: Formattable<T> = ?> -> Formattable<List<T, M>> {
    format = (v, w) -> () {
        w.write("[")
        for (i := 0; i < v.count; i += 1) {
            if (i > 0) { w.write(", ") }
            F.format(v.items[i], w)
        }
        w.write("]")
    }
}


// ============================================================================
// std.fmt.str
// ============================================================================

// A Writer, structurally. No closures, no capture, memcpy-movable.
StringWriter :: <M: Allocator> (mem: M) -> self {
    buf := List<u8>(mem)

    write_bytes :: (&self, p: ptr<u8>, n: u32) -> () {
        for (i := 0; i < n; i += 1) { self.buf.push(p[i]) }
    }

    finish :: (&self) -> slice<u8> { slice(self.buf.items, self.buf.count) }
}

to_string :: <T, M: Allocator, F: Formattable<T> = ?> (mem: M, v: T) -> slice<u8> {
    b := StringWriter(mem)
    F.format(v, &b)          // &b coerces to ptr<Writer>; the fat pointer is
    b.finish()               // built right here, and only here
}


// ============================================================================
// main
// ============================================================================

main :: () -> () {
    mem := Heap()                      // zero-sized
    xs  := List<u8>(mem)               // M = Heap; every alloc call is direct
    defer xs.deinit()

    xs.push(2)
    xs.push(1)

    io.out.write(to_string(mem, xs[0]))              // "2"
    io.out.write(to_string(mem, xs))                 // "[2, 1]"

    // Override the solver by naming the dictionary. It is a compile-time
    // argument passed by name, so it uses `=`, like any other default.
    io.out.write(to_string<F = hex_u8>(mem, xs[0]))  // "02"
    io.out.write(to_string<F = hex_u8>(mem, xs))     // "[02, 01]"

    sort(&xs)                          // Ord<u8> from the pool
    sort<O = reverse_u8>(&xs)          // by hand

    match (xs.pop()) {
        some (v) { io.out.write(to_string(mem, v)) }
        none     { io.err.write("empty") }
    }

    // Static composition: Tracking<Heap> is a distinct type, calls still direct.
    audited := Tracking(Heap())
    zs := List<u8>(audited)
    defer zs.deinit()

    // Dynamic: the annotation is the coercion. Same List, no second decl,
    // no `dyn`. Now the calls go through a fat pointer.
    arena := Arena(page, 4096)
    boxed : ptr<Allocator> := &arena
    ys := List<u8>(boxed)
    defer ys.deinit()

    // A closure is what is left over: a block holding `m` with one member
    // named `()`. A closure is a struct; that is the whole point.
    print_u8 :: <M: Allocator> (m: M) (v: u8) -> () { io.out.write(to_string(m, v)) }
    p := print_u8(mem)
    p(7)                               // -> p.()(7)

    // ...and since it is a block with a `()` member, it satisfies Fn<u8, ()>
    // structurally, and erases exactly like every other shape.
    f : ptr<Fn<u8, ()>> := &p
    f(8)
}


// ============================================================================
// NOTE 1 -- shapes vs dictionaries, and why one syntax is enough.
//
// Rust conflates these and pays for it with orphan rules. Go has only shapes.
// Haskell has only dictionaries. You can have both, told apart by one word:
//
//   SHAPE       every hole takes self. An allocator, a writer, a socket --
//               things where the abstraction is a property of the object and
//               there is exactly one sensible answer per type. Satisfied
//               structurally by any type with matching methods, so it needs
//               no registration. Coerce to ptr<Shape> for dynamic dispatch.
//
//   DICTIONARY  no hole takes self. Ord<u8>, Formattable<u8> -- things where
//               several answers exist and the caller must be able to choose. A
//               free-standing value, defaulted by the solver, overridden by
//               name. Works on primitives and foreign types, since nothing has
//               to be attached to them.
//
// The test is not stylistic: can there legitimately be two of these for one
// type? Two orderings of u8, yes -> dictionary. Two ways for an Arena to
// allocate, no -> shape.
//
// It is a property of the BLOCK, not of each hole. A block with one self hole
// and one non-self hole is an error; it would have no dispatch story and no
// coercion, and admitting it buys nothing.
//
// Anything that must AGREE belongs in ONE block. A hash and an equals solved
// as two independent `= ?` params can be defaulted inconsistently -- a
// case-folding hash with a case-sensitive equals is a silently broken map that
// no type error catches. Grouping is free here, because a dictionary is just a
// block.
//
// Neither is a new construct. Both are a block with unfilled holes.
//
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// NOTE 2 -- static vs dynamic is a type, not a keyword.
//
//     List<u8>(mem)      M = Heap            direct calls, 0-byte field
//     List<u8>(audited)  M = Tracking<Heap>  direct calls, still monomorphic
//     List<u8>(boxed)    M = ptr<Allocator>  fat pointer, indirect calls
//
// One declaration, three behaviors. The cost of the first two: they are
// different types and cannot share an array. That is the trade, and it is now
// visible in the code instead of hidden in a keyword.
//
// The trade gets much worse for RECURSIVE types, which is now the largest
// open problem in the design. See OPEN.
//
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// NOTE 3 -- shapes contain holes and nothing else.
//
// An earlier draft let a shape carry provided methods -- `make :: <T> ...`
// with a body, non-virtual, generic, overridable by an implementer. It bought
// `a.make<T>(n)` and cost three things:
//
//   - `:` vs `::` had to be read carefully inside every shape
//   - an implementer could override a provided method, and that override
//     would silently vanish the moment the value was coerced to ptr<Shape>,
//     because non-virtual methods are not in the table
//   - the shape's author owned the convenience layer forever
//
// Moving them to `mem` costs nothing at the call site, because UFCS makes
// `mem.make<T>(capacity)` read the same either way. All three problems go.
//
// What is genuinely lost: a Pool cannot specialize `make` with per-type free
// lists. If that need ever becomes real, the answer is a separate
// `TypedAllocator<T>` shape -- monomorphic holes per instantiation, so the
// specialization is a vtable slot and survives coercion -- not a default.
//
// Background, since the tension is real for anything with a table:
//     GENERIC . OVERRIDABLE . VIRTUAL  --  pick two.
// Shape-level generics escape it by multiplying vtables; that is why
// Iterator<T> is right and Allocator<T> is not.
//
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// NOTE 4 -- what lives in the block, and what UFCS may do.
//
// Methods in the block get T, M and the fields in scope for free, and they are
// the only thing shape matching looks at.
//
//     INTRINSIC   declared in the block. Counts for shape satisfaction. May
//                 be virtual. `push`, `alloc`, `write_bytes`.
//     EXTRINSIC   a free function called via UFCS. Convenience only, never
//                 satisfies a shape. `make`, `drop`, `write`.
//
// The second half of that rule is load-bearing, and follows directly from UFCS
// resolving the free function in the CALLER's scope. If UFCS could satisfy a
// shape, then "does Heap satisfy Allocator" would depend on what is imported,
// and two modules could coerce the same value and build different vtables.
//
// With that split you do get extension methods -- add anything to a type you
// do not own, it just cannot participate in a shape.
//
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// NOTE 5 -- derived instances are ordinary defs.
//
// The pattern-on-the-left form
//     Formattable<List<T,M>> :: <T, M, F: Formattable<T> = ?> { ... }
// was a second, unification-shaped mechanism. Writing it as an anonymous def
// with a return type makes it an ordinary declaration; the solver matches the
// return type, which it already had to do for `= ?` params.
//
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// NOTE 6 -- why `operator` had to go.
//
// It was the language's only two-token name, and the only place the word
// appeared. Naming the member `[]` costs nothing and removes a keyword.
//
// It also pays for itself twice, because the same move names the member `()`,
// and that is what a closure is. `print_u8 :: <M: Allocator> (m: M) (v: u8)`
// desugars to
//
//     print_u8 :: <M: Allocator> (m: M) -> self { (): (&self, v: u8) -> () {...} }
//
// -- an ordinary `-> self` block, whose runtime params are its captured fields,
// exactly as everywhere else. Two questions that were open in the previous
// draft answer themselves: `print_u8(mem)` has its OWN type (so it is free),
// and it becomes a shared type precisely when you write `ptr<Fn<u8, ()>>`,
// which is the same coercion as `ptr<Allocator>` and not a second mechanism.
//
// ============================================================================
// CHANGED IN THIS REVISION -- all of it found by writing src/json.z, a JSON
// parser plus the containers it needs, in this syntax. That file keeps the
// full log, including what was tried and rejected.
//
//   ADDED    `-> oneof`, the sum projection, and holes-are-quantified-by-the-
//            projection. A JSON value is a sum; so is Option, Result, a hash
//            slot, and a token. The previous draft could express none of them.
//   ADDED    `match`, `_`, `try`, and `defer` (which the old draft already used
//            at line 283 without introducing it).
//   ADDED    `-> {a, b}` as visibility. It was already in the grammar.
//   ADDED    the instantiation rule (declaration / +<> = type / +() = value),
//            and "a type in value position is the solver's default instance".
//            Both were relied on and neither was stated.
//   ADDED    a shape in `<>` is static, in `()` is erased.
//   CHANGED  `to_string<F: hex_u8>` -> `<F = hex_u8>`. The old spelling
//            contradicted "`:` is type ascription and nothing else".
//   CHANGED  `operator []` -> `[]`. See NOTE 6.
//   CHANGED  `List.pop` returns Option<T>.
//   CHANGED  shape-vs-dictionary is a property of the block, not of each hole.
//   FIXED    `io.out` is now declared; the UFCS comment on List.items was
//            wrong; UFCS scope resolution is now stated.
//
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// OPEN
//
//   - COHERENCE. Two anonymous `-> Ord<u8>` in different files is an
//     ambiguity. Error at the use site, or first-wins, or one-per-module? Still
//     the biggest unanswered question in the file.
//
//   - MONOMORPHISATION AND RECURSIVE TYPES, and now the closest rival for that
//     title. A JSON value parameterised by its allocator is `Json<M>`, its
//     arrays are `List<Json<M>, M>`, and M then appears in the parser, the
//     printer, the error type and every user function. Two documents built from
//     different allocators are DIFFERENT TYPES and cannot be compared, merged
//     or stored together. The only livable answer found so far is to erase at
//     the type declaration (`ptr<Allocator>`), which means the headline feature
//     is unavailable for exactly the data structures people write most.
//
//   - A RECURSIVE DICTIONARY INSTANCE CANNOT PROPAGATE THE CALLER'S CHOICE.
//     `Formattable<Json>` prints its children by calling `Formattable<Json>
//     .format`, which is the SOLVER'S DEFAULT -- so `to_string<F = pretty>`
//     picks `pretty` for the outermost value and the default for every child.
//     The override stops at depth 1, silently.
//
//     `Formattable<List<T,M>>` above dodges this without noticing, because a
//     list's elements are a DIFFERENT type and it can thread `F` by hand. A
//     recursive type would have to name itself -- `F: Formattable<Json> = ?`
//     inside the Formattable<Json> instance -- and there is no spelling for
//     "the instance I am". Dictionaries have no `self`; that is what makes
//     them free-standing and it is what breaks here.
//
//     Shapes are immune: a ptr<Shape> carries its vtable, so recursion through
//     the value keeps the choice. This is the first thing found that the
//     shape/dictionary split makes WORSE rather than better, and it is the
//     same shape of problem as the recursive-monomorphisation item above --
//     both are about recursion, neither is about syntax.
//
//   - `_` DOES NOT REACH PROJECTION POSITION, so it half-fixes the plumbing
//     parameter. `sort` is clean because `M` appears only in a parameter type.
//     A wrapper is not: `iter :: <T, M> (xs: ptr<List<T, M>>) -> ListIter<T, M>`
//     must name the type to spell its own return.
//
//   - MUTABILITY. `get` and `put` both take `&self`. Nothing says `get` does
//     not write, so nothing can alias-check, and a `ptr<Allocator>` handed to a
//     printer is a fully mutable handle. `::`/`:=` already encode immutable vs
//     mutable for BINDINGS; there is no counterpart for POINTEES, and no
//     candidate spelling yet that does not cost a keyword.
//
//   - THE DISCRIMINANT IS NOT A VALUE. "same variant, ignore the payload" --
//     what a parser's `expect(Token.colon())` wants -- cannot be said.
//
//   - REFUTABLE MATCH. When `probe` has already proved the slot is `live`, the
//     match must still name `empty` and `dead`. Every container grows a tail of
//     dead arms. Wanted: a single-arm assertion form.
//
//   - INFERENCE DIRECTION. `Result<Token, Error>.ok(t)` at every return is
//     unreadable, and the fix is to infer the sum's type from the enclosing
//     projection -- inference flowing INWARD from the signature into the body,
//     which the language does not otherwise do. Decide it deliberately.
//
//   - ORDER INDEPENDENCE is required and unstated. Recursive types force it
//     (a slot references an entry declared after it), and so does the solver,
//     which must have seen the whole file. This interacts with the next item.
//
//   - FORWARD DECLARATIONS. Bodiless means hole, so a hand-written .zh full of
//     `func insert_item<T>(...);` would make the module abstract rather than
//     declaring it. Headers have to be generated from implementations, not
//     maintained beside them. Probably the right answer anyway, but it is a
//     real change to how the project is laid out.
//
//   - FALLIBLE CONSTRUCTION. `-> Result<self, E>` is adopted above, but its
//     interaction with field-initialiser order is unspecified: which fields
//     exist when the failing step runs, and what is destroyed on the err path?
//
//   - Does `capacity :: 10` (compile-time field, 0 bytes) make List<u8> with
//     cap 10 a different type from cap 20? Zig says yes.
//
//   - Is the ptr<Shape> coercion implicit at call sites (`F.format(v, &b)`) or
//     does it need the annotation, as `boxed` does? Implicit is nicer to write
//     and hides a representation change from direct calls to indirect ones.
//
//   - `Fn`, `Fn2`, `Fn3`. A numbered family of names, the only one in the
//     language, and unavoidable without variadic compile-time args.
// ============================================================================
